**Read:** [Cleanroom programming and energy — Tschudi, Sartor and Xu](../sources/Cleanroom-Programming-and-Energy-LBNL.lingua#r_36R5RS6HEWBTYN4SHCVPQNP12X) in full; [Air-cleaning systems — DOE-HDBK-1169-2022](../sources/Air-Cleaning-Handbook-DOE.lingua#r_09QPEZ76E9W6HS7R55X2K8TB3X), chapters 3 and 4, retaining its nuclear-facility scope; [Multizone airflow and contaminants — Dols and Polidoro, CONTAM 3.4](../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46), introductory/tutorial material and the complete chapter 8 theory. Read the full [Demand-controlled cleanroom filtration — Faulkner, Fisk and Walton](../sources/Demand-Controlled-Cleanroom-Filtration-Faulkner-Fisk-Walton.lingua#r_2W1ADE030W9RFA8ATDKSYVT6XP) experiment as the quantitative control example.

The cleaned part now enters an environment built and maintained by people. Air, surfaces, movement, water, electricity, heat and information connect the room to the wider production system. Begin with the process that needs protection and follow each connection to its sources and destinations.

The progression moves from layout and barriers to air balances, filters, fans, pressure networks, transfers, thermal conditions and control. Three investigations then combine air-cleaning performance, pressure/transfer behavior and resource use.

**Work:** Draw one process, its sensitive region, people and material routes, and all utility connections. Mark the observations that would show preserved function. Keep the drawing as a working model and revise it as the facility's mechanisms become clearer.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
**Continue in clean manufacturing:** [Translate the process into observable facility requirements](Translate-the-process-into-observable-facility-requirements.lingua#r_38A200F1H46XAJ3DRTKHZ183SV).

